Steven Warrick

Steven Warrick
County: Santa Rosa County
Type: Circuit
Divisions: Juvenile Dependency, Juvenile Delinquency, Probate & Guardianship
Phone: 850-981-5540
Fax: N/A
Judicial Assistant: Tiffany Coulter, Tiffany.Coulter@Flcourts1.Gov
Address: Santa Rosa County Courthouse, 4025 Avalon Blvd., Milton, Florida 32583

DIVISION PREFERENCES

This is a brief overview of general information and policies for Division D.

CONTACTING JUDGE WARRICK’S OFFICE

Email is the preferred method of communication for Judge Warrick’s office.

SUBMITTING PROPOSED ORDERS

ALL proposed orders are to be submitted through the Florida Courts ePortal, by way of the “Proposed Order” function and selecting Santa Rosa in the drop-down list and selecting the correct Judge. Proposed orders must be in MS Word Format. Judge Warrick does not require a cover letter. Document should be titled the same as the title of the order you are submitting. It is not necessary to include “proposed” in the title. In the event a proposed order has an exhibit or other attachment that can’t be accomplished using MS Word, you may email the order directly to the judicial assistant at tiffany.coulter@flcourts1.gov.

Below is the preferred signature block.

DONE AND ORDERED in Milton, Santa Rosa County, Florida.

Steven C. Warrick

Circuit Court Judge

All proposed orders must include as the last paragraph (before DONE AND ORDERED) the following statement:

In cases wherein one party is unrepresented (pro se), it is the responsibility of the sole attorney in the case to serve within five business days this [order or judgment] upon any pro se party who does not have access to and is not a registered user of the Florida Court ePortal.

Once the proposed order or judgment has been signed, it will be efiled. It is the responsibility of the submitting party/counsel to provide copies to any party not included on the service list. Do not include a certificate of service to be completed by the judicial assistant.

To check the status of a proposed order, you should first check the Santa Rosa County Clerk’s website to see if it has been docketed. If the order is not listed, you may email the judicial assistant.

SCHEDULING A HEARING

To schedule a hearing, please email the judicial assistant and include the opposing party/attorney, request available dates and times and indicate the amount of time needed. All Probate/Guardianship hearings are by Zoom, unless in-person is specifically requested.

NOTICE OF HEARING

The moving party must prepare, serve, and file the Notice of Hearing. The Notice should include all matters to be heard by the Court; the length of time for the hearing; the location of the hearing; and whether it is in person or by Zoom.

ZOOM INFORMATION FOR HEARINGS

If the hearing is to be held via Zoom, the following Zoom information needs to be included in the Notice of Hearing:

ZOOM INSTRUCTIONS:

You may join this meeting from a Computer, Smartphone, or Tablet. You will be prompted upon joining the meeting as to which device you want to use for audio. You may either select “Join with computer audio” to use the built-in microphone and speakers on your device, or you may select “phone call” and dial in on a separate telephone for audio.

If your video does not automatically turn on upon joining the meeting, you may need to click in the bottom left corner to “Start Video.”

Please use the highlighted link below to connect to the Zoom meeting. You may join the meeting anytime beforehand to test your speakers and microphone using the built-in test feature. Please be advised that this is a court event and will be digitally recorded. All participants will be recorded from the time you log in until you exit.

*Please remember to mute your microphone when you are not speaking.*

*Please connect to the Zoom meeting 10 minutes before the trial begins.*

Join Zoom Meeting from Computer

https://zoom.us/j/9847613263

Meeting ID: 984 761 3263

Dial-in from telephone

+1 312 626 6799 US (Primary)

+1 929 205 6099 US (Secondary)

CANCELLING A HEARING

Upon determining that a hearing is no longer necessary, please cancel the time set aside with the judicial assistant as soon as possible. The ONLY way this office knows of a cancellation is through your notification. If a Notice of Hearing has been filed for said hearing, then a Notice of Cancellation of Hearing is required to remove the hearing from the Court’s schedule.

MOTIONS TO WITHDRAW/SUBSTITUTION OF COUNSEL

In accordance with Florida Rule of General Practice and Judicial Administration 2.505, written consent of the client must be filed with the Clerk of Court and opposing counsel (if any) must have no objection to the withdrawal/substitution. If the client has not signed a consent and/or opposing counsel has an objection to the withdrawal, a hearing is required.

PROBATE AND GUARDIANSHIP REQUIRED CHECKLISTS/AFFIDAVITS

Probate Checklists/Affidavits required for Santa Rosa County:

Checklist for Opening Formal Administration

Checklist for Closing Formal Administration

Checklist for Summary Administration

Checklist for Determining Homestead Status of Real Property (Required in both Formal and Summary Administration matters

Affidavit of Heirs (Required in ALL Intestate matters, both Formal and Summary)

Guardianship Checklists required for Santa Rosa County:

Checklist and Certification-Attorney and Guardian Fees

Checklist and Certification-Discharge

Checklists and Affidavits are to be efiled once completed.

CONCLUSION

If you have any questions regarding any of the above information, please feel free to contact our office by emailing Tiffany Coulter at tiffany.coulter@flcourts1.gov or calling 850-981-5540.

About Us

The First Judicial Circuit is one of 20 judicial circuits in Florida. Our courts serve the people of Escambia, Okaloosa, Santa Rosa, and Walton Counties.

